From 10e72df09f311cad0be6d83ec4fb00e77f9f4466 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ethan Lee <ethantlee21@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:25:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat: let a client claim leadership without sending input
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

zmx hands leadership to any client that sends real user input, which is the
right rule for a human at a keyboard. It leaves no way for a GUI frontend
showing one session in two panes to say "size the pty from this one" when the
user clicks or focuses it: the only lever is typing, and the keystroke would
reach their running program.

Adds an IPC Claim tag and an APC sequence the client excises from stdin. APC
because no keyboard produces one and terminals discard unknown APC, so a stray
sequence reaching a program that predates this is inert. The Tag enum is
non-exhaustive by design, so an old daemon ignores the message.

The filter is deliberately not shaped like isCtrlBackslash, which returns a
bool and lets its caller drop the entire read. A detach key is a keystroke and
arrives alone; a claim is written programmatically and can land in the same
4096-byte read as whatever the user is typing, so an all-or-nothing match would
swallow their keystrokes. ClaimFilter excises occurrences and forwards the rest.

It also carries a partial sequence between reads, since a pty write can be
split anywhere. The carry has a floor of three bytes: a trailing one- or
two-byte partial is a bare ESC or ESC `_`, and Escape is pressed constantly in
a TUI — holding it back until the user's next keystroke would be visible input
lag. Three bytes is already a sequence no keyboard produces. The cost is a
claim split within its first two bytes being dropped rather than rejoined,
which needs the split to land exactly there and which the next keystroke
recovers from anyway.

Also fixes handleDetachAll leaving a stale leader_client_fd. closeClient nils
it per client, but that path clears the list wholesale, so the next client to
attach found it non-null: handleInit neither made it leader nor resized the
pty, and the session stayed at the detached client's size until someone typed.
Worse if the kernel recycled the fd number, which makes an unrelated client
leader by coincidence.

test/claim_leader.py drives two real PTYs at different sizes to prove the two
properties the in-process tests cannot reach: a second client attaching does
not resize the pty, and a claim does — with no keystroke reaching the program.
